  "raw": "\n\nBut now Californians face another problem: About 1 million acres of trees are killed each year from various coccidiosis and leaf necrosis diseases that grow in forests understory -- areas already coddled by years of drought that dash vegetation's ability to protect itself. Some researchers point out it could take much longer than fire season to recover.\n\n\"The kinds of affect this has upon watersheds, residents' water supply and the ecosystem is not just related to water,\" said Jan van der Werff, who works on forest health at California State University, Chico and the UC remote sensing lab in Maywood. \"These are all weather effects under the umbrella of drought.\"\n\nDrought is an official statewide disaster, but state officials point out they will likely loosen their definition as improvements continue in recent weeks. A more refined definition represents drought under at least two criteria -- very low total flows between reservoir reservoirs and the ecosystem this spring compared with average summer flow rates since 1940 (",
